# Phase 4A doctor Android pilot — non-destructive, and scoped

`PHASE4A-DOCTOR-ANDROID-PILOT-PREPARATION-1`. Read this before touching the
pilot, the enforcement flag, or the device model.

## The pilot, in full

| Item | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Doctor | drg Karmila |
| Branch | Cabang Sunu |
| Device | logical label `PHASE4A_PILOT_TABLET_01` |
| Devices / branches / doctors | 1 / 1 / 1 |

A hardware serial, IMEI, MAC or Android ID is never committed. The logical label
is the only device reference in source control.

## Phase 4A is non-destructive. This is not negotiable by reading the fleet model.

- `factory_reset_required` = **false**
- `device_owner_required` = **false**
- `full_kiosk_required` = **false**
- `pilot_model` = `self_owned_non_destructive`

The owner refused a factory reset for this pilot, and the tablet is already in
service. Android grants Device Owner only on a device with no accounts on it,
which in practice means immediately after a reset.

`fleet_model` is still `self_owned_device_owner` and that is correct — a
dedicated clinic tablet should be wiped and locked down. It is simply not
available for a pilot on a tablet somebody is already working on. Do not read
the fleet model as permission to wipe the pilot device.

## Six acceptance checks are DEFERRED, not deleted

`device_owner_established`, `lock_task_active`, `home_escape_blocked`,
`recents_escape_blocked`, `external_browser_escape_blocked` and
`reboot_returns_to_clinic_app` are Device Owner and lock-task properties. They
live in `phase_4a.deferred_to_dedicated_device_phase` and remain owed by whoever
provisions a dedicated device.

The canonical set is `scanner.dedicated_device_kiosk_checks`, and the readiness
gate proves the deferral against **that**, not against the deferral list — a
proof that walks the list it is proving can be defeated by editing the list.

## Rule 147, and the thing the deferral actually gave up

Lock task was also what physically kept a doctor away from a browser. Without it,
**app-only is a server-side decision and nothing else.**

The boundary does hold there: `DoctorAppLoginGate` denies a browser session from
the **absence** of a server-verified device binding, re-checks it on every
protected request, and only redemption of a server-minted ticket signed over a
single-use nonce can create one. Leaving the app grants nothing.

But it holds **only while enforcement is armed for that doctor**. So on a
non-destructive pilot, "app-only" is true if and only if the pilot scope is armed.
Claiming app-only without an armed scope is claiming a property you do not have.

## Enforcement must be scoped before it is armed

`doctor.trusted_device_enforcement` is one boolean and its registry entry says
what arming it does: it denies browser login to **every** account holding the
Doctor role. `App\Support\Android\AndroidDoctorEnforcementScope` narrows that.

| Configuration | Enforced for |
| --- | --- |
| `pilot` with a valid `doctor_user_id` | that one doctor |
| `pilot` with no target | **nobody** |
| `unscoped` with `global_permitted` | every doctor |
| `unscoped` without `global_permitted` | **nobody** |
| an unrecognised mode | **nobody** |

The scope only ever **narrows**. It never widens, it never reads a request, and
it never invents a target.

**Order of operations, always:** set the scope, clear the config cache, verify,
*then* arm the flag. Arming the flag while the scope covers nobody enforces
nothing — it looks like protection and is not. `android:phase4a-pilot-readiness`
FAILS on exactly that state; run it.

## Two files, on purpose

- `config/android_release.php` is the governance record and **never reads the
  environment** — the release readiness gate must be safe on a fork pull request,
  and that is held as a bright line rather than a per-key argument. Do not write
  the environment-read function-call spelling in that file, not even in a comment:
  the governance suite pattern-matches it.
- `config/doctor_device_enforcement.php` carries the two host values: the mode and
  the pilot doctor's user id. User ids are not portable between this repository and
  production, so the committed target is null and null enforces nobody.
- `global_permitted` lives in the governance record, so **a fleet-wide clinical
  lockout is not reachable by setting a variable on a host.** It costs a reviewed
  source-control change. Keep that asymmetry.

## Why the scope fails toward "enforce nobody"

This is the opposite of the usual deny-by-default reflex, so it is deliberate.

Denying here does not withhold data from an attacker — RBAC, branch, room and
consent gates all still apply, and a doctor still needs their password. It
withholds a doctor's ability to reach their patients. A mistyped variable that
locked every doctor out of every branch is a clinical incident; the same mistake
resolving to "enforce nobody" leaves production as it is today and grants nothing
new.

The cost is a possible silent no-op, so it is made loud: `enforcement_inactive`
FAILS when the flag is armed while the scope covers nobody, the command prints
`ENFORCEMENT_SCOPE_USABLE` directly under `ENFORCEMENT_FLAG_ARMED`, and checklist
step **F8** requires proving a non-pilot doctor can still log in through a browser.

## Preparation is not activation. Approved is not distributed.

`phase_4a.activation_boundary` records eight claims that must all be false:
`apk_distributed`, `apk_installed`, `tablet_touched`, `adb_used`,
`device_enrolled`, `pilot_activated`, `pilot_browser_denial_active`,
`global_enforcement_active`.

The preparation lifecycle stops at `ready_for_pilot`. An unrecognised state is a
FAIL, never a pass — that is how an unset decision passes for a made one.

## Before the APK goes near the tablet

Exact, full-length comparisons. A prefix match on a digest or a fingerprint is not
a verification.

- SHA-256 `ab3e30df111ca3cfb6aa5efeb37dde1b3624e822c88134065c2c314a2fd10a03`
- signer `79db269b7cd38e920b80efbcf2f59142721f1e57924d3048d07a862f34fea2d9`,
  compared against the pin in **source control**, never the copy in the manifest —
  whoever can swap the APK can swap the file beside it
- `com.daengtisia.clinic`, versionName `0.3.0-phase3`, versionCode `1`

## Update in place, and never uninstall as a reflex

Same application id, same signer, strictly higher versionCode. A signer mismatch
cannot update in place, and uninstalling to force it **destroys the Android
Keystore device identity and the enrolment with it**. Clearing app data does the
same. Installation alone restores no trust. Uninstall appears in no routine
rollback route.

## The pilot has to leave a trail

`phase_4a.audit_events_required` lists the eight events the activation sprint owes:
enrolment requested, PENDING created, approved, **rejected with a reason**, device
disabled, device revoked, **session invalidated with a reason**, and pilot
enforcement scope changed. Three of those are mandatory
(`phase_4a.audit_events_mandatory`) and the readiness gate FAILS if they are not
declared — an approval with no named approver and a rejection with no recorded
reason are exactly the two decisions that get re-litigated later.

Record the logical device label. Never a serial, an IMEI, a MAC or an Android ID,
and never a challenge nonce or signature beyond what the decision needed.

## Running the pilot

Follow `docs/runbooks/android-phase4a-pilot-activation-checklist.md` line by line.
Preparation required no signing key, no tablet and no ADB, and the production
signing key stays out of git, CI and the VPS.
